      "dom_id": "question-85881",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "An experiment consists of tossing a fair coin until it comes up heads. What is the probability that we get our first head on the $3$rd toss? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of times the coin is tossed until it comes up heads. Since the chance of getting a head in every toss is $50%,$ the number of tosses until it comes up heads can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=50%=\\frac{50}{100}=0.5.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.5).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.5)^{x-1}(0.5) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{x-1}(0.5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that head appears in the $3$rd toss is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{3-1}(0.5) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{2}(0.5) \\\\  & ≈0.125\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
