      "name": null,
      "problem": "In a town, blood tests are being carried out on all inhabitants to determine whether they have caught a virus. On average, $30%$ of the inhabitants test positive for the virus. What is the probability that the first positive test result will be made on the $3$rd test or later?",
      "worked_solution": "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of inhabitants checked until the first infected patient is found. Since the chance of a single patient being infected is $30%,$ the number of inhabitants checked until the first infected patient is found can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=30%=\\frac{30}{100}=0.3.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.3).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.3)^{x-1}(0.3) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{x-1}(0.3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=1-P(X<3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{1-1}(0.3)+(0.7)^{2-1}(0.3) \\\\  & =(0.3)+(0.7)(0.3) \\\\  & =[1+0.7](0.3) \\\\  & =0.51.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding the first positive patient on the $3$rd test or later is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & =1-0.51 \\\\  & =0.49.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
